# The files every machine touches.
#
# CONTRIBUTING.md and DEVELOPING.md both say these six need a word in
# #staff-general before they are edited, because a change in any of them lands on
# everybody else's work without warning. Until now that rule was prose in two documents with nothing
# behind it: nothing asked, nothing checked, and it was forgotten as often as
# it was kept.
#
# A leading slash pins the pattern to the repository root, the same way a
# gitignore pattern works. Without it, tools/app/Shell.cs would also match any
# Shell.cs in any folder added later.
#
# WHAT THIS DOES AND DOES NOT DO. GitHub requests a review from the owner named
# here when a pull request touches the file, and never asks a pull request's
# own author. So a pull request one of the team opens on Shell.cs asks the
# others, which is exactly the intent. The team is the table in
# CONTRIBUTING.md; somebody added there is added here too, and
# tools\test-branch-rules.js checks the two agree.
#
# It is a request and nothing more. Branch protection on main, read off GitHub
# on 23 September: enforce_admins is on, so every change, Patrick's included,
# reaches main through a pull request and nobody pushes there; the one
# required check is "Launcher builds"; required approving reviews is 0; and
# Require review from Code Owners is off. So a pull request can be merged by
# its own author as soon as its build is green, whether or not anybody named
# here has read it.
#
# Switching the code owner review on is Patrick's decision, and it is not
# free. With enforce_admins on, a pull request of his that touches /.github/
# could never merge, because he is its only owner and GitHub does not let an
# author approve their own. Every one of his that touches the shared files
# below would wait for somebody else. The settings change without a commit, so read
# them rather than this comment:
#
#     gh api repos/EvilPatrick06/Thornswood/branches/main/protection
#
# A mistyped path or a handle without push access is ignored in silence, which
# is why the check is worth running after any edit here:
#
#     gh api repos/EvilPatrick06/Thornswood/codeowners/errors

